      "text": "# Producer-Consumer Queue Design Review\n\n## Design Analysis\n\n### 1. Bounded Capacity\nA bounded queue prevents unbounded memory growth by limiting the number of items that can be buffered. When the queue is full, producers block (or wait) until space becomes available. This provides **backpressure** \u2014 a natural flow-control mechanism that prevents fast producers from overwhelming slow consumers.\n\n**Key considerations:**\n- Capacity must be \u2265 1\n- Blocking semantics vs. non-blocking `put_nowait`/`get_nowait`\n- Fairness: FIFO ordering ensures no starvation\n\n### 2. Cancellation\nCancellation allows in-flight operations (blocking `put` or `get`) to be aborted when a task is cancelled (e.g., via `asyncio.CancelledError` or thread interruption). Without proper cancellation handling:\n- A blocked producer/consumer may never wake up\n- Resources leak\n- Shutdown hangs\n\n**Key considerations:**\n- Use `asyncio.Event` or `threading.Event` for cooperative cancellation\n- Ensure `finally` blocks release locks/resources\n- Propagate cancellation to waiting tasks\n\n### 3. Shutdown\nGraceful shutdown ensures:\n- All pending items are processed (or explicitly discarded)\n- No",
