      "text": "# Practical Guide: Investigating a Slow Distributed Inference Service\n\nDistributed inference systems (e.g., LLM serving, large-scale vision models) are complex stacks involving hardware, OS, network, and application logic. A \"slow\" service is rarely caused by a single factor; it is usually a symptom of a bottleneck in one of four layers: **Compute (CPU/GPU)**, **Memory (HBM/DRAM)**, **Communication (Network/Interconnect)**, or **Orchestration (Scheduler/Queue)**.\n\nThis guide provides a systematic, experiment-driven approach to isolating the root cause.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Establish a Reliable Baseline & Avoid Misleading Benchmarks\n\nBefore debugging, you must ensure your measurements are valid. Most \"slow\" reports are artifacts of poor benchmarking.\n\n### Common Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them\n\n| Pitfall | Why It Misleads | Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cold Start Effects** | First requests suffer from JIT compilation, cache misses, and model loading. | **Warm up** the service with 10\u201350 dummy requests before measuring. |\n| **Client-Side Bottleneck** | The client generating requests is slower",
