      "text": "# Producer-Consumer Queue Design Review & Implementation\n\n## Design Review\n\n### 1. Bounded Capacity\n\nA bounded queue prevents unbounded memory growth by limiting the number of items that can be buffered. When the queue is full, producers must **block** (or fail) until space becomes available. This provides:\n\n- **Backpressure**: Producers are naturally throttled when consumers can't keep up.\n- **Memory safety**: Prevents OOM in scenarios where production outpaces consumption.\n- **Predictable resource usage**: The maximum memory footprint is known upfront.\n\nThe classic approach uses two semaphores (or a condition variable with a mutex):\n- `not_full`: Counts available slots (initialized to `capacity`).\n- `not_empty`: Counts available items (initialized to `0`).\n\n### 2. Cancellation\n\nCancellation allows in-flight operations to be aborted gracefully. Key considerations:\n\n- **Cooperative cancellation**: Producers/consumers check a cancellation flag or respond to exceptions.\n- **Timeout-based cancellation**: `queue.get(timeout=...)` and `queue.put(timeout=...)` allow callers to abandon blocking waits.\n- **Context propagation**: In async contexts, cancellation tokens or `asyncio.CancelledError`",
