      "text": "# Practical Guide: Investigating a Slow Distributed Inference Service\n\nDistributed inference systems (e.g., LLM serving, large-scale vision models) are complex stacks involving networking, GPU/TPU hardware, CPU orchestration, and memory hierarchies. A \"slow\" service is rarely caused by a single factor; it is usually a symptom of a bottleneck in one of four domains: **Compute**, **Memory**, **Communication**, or **Orchestration**.\n\nThis guide provides a systematic approach to diagnosing performance issues, isolating bottlenecks, and avoiding common benchmarking pitfalls.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Establish a Baseline & Define \"Slow\"\n\nBefore debugging, you must define what \"slow\" means. In distributed inference, latency is not a single number.\n\n### Key Metrics to Track\n1.  **Time to First Token (TTFT):** Critical for user experience. Measures the time from request arrival to the first output token.\n2.  **Time Per Output Token (TPOT):** Measures the steady-state generation speed.\n3.  **End-to-End Latency:** Total time from request to full response.\n4.  **Throughput:** Requests per second (RPS) or tokens per second",
